/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Compare Structures with Unions, detailing memory overlap, type punning, and memory size calculation formulas.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Structures vs Unions in C:) Tj
T*
(   1. Fundamental Distinction:) Tj
T*
(   - struct: Allocates separate, distinct memory space for each member. All members exist) Tj
T*
(   simultaneously and independently.) Tj
T*
(   Total Size = Sum of member sizes + internal/tail alignment padding.) Tj
T*
(   - union: Allocates a single shared memory location for all members. All members occupy the) Tj
T*
(   exact same base address; only ONE member can be actively stored at any given moment.) Tj
T*
(   Total Size = Size of its largest member \(plus any padding required for the largest member's) Tj
T*
(   alignment\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Memory Footprint Comparison:) Tj
T*
(   struct S { int i; double d; char c; };) Tj
T*
(   Size: 4 \(i\) + 4 \(pad\) + 8 \(d\) + 1 \(c\) + 7 \(tail pad\) = 24 bytes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   union U { int i; double d; char c; };) Tj
T*
(   Size: Largest member is double \(8 bytes\). Total size = 8 bytes!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Type Punning via Unions:) Tj
T*
(   Type punning inspects the raw binary representation of one data type as another data type:) Tj
T*
(   union FloatBits {) Tj
T*
(   float f;) Tj
T*
(   unsigned int bits;) Tj
T*
(   };) Tj
T*
(   Assigning fb.f = 1.0f; and reading fb.bits reveals the exact IEEE 754 bit pattern \(0x3F800000\)!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

