(   [Problem Statement]: Explain stream buffering modes in C: Full Buffering \(_IOFBF\), Line Buffering \(_IOLBF\), and No Buffering \(_IONBF\) via setvbuf\(\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Stream Buffering Modes and setvbuf\(\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Stream Buffering Mechanics:) Tj
T*
(   I/O system calls \(read/write\) are slow kernel context switches. User-space buffers bundle many) Tj
T*
(   byte operations into large block transfers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Three Buffering Modes:) Tj
T*
(   - _IOFBF \(Full Buffering\): Flushes buffer to physical device only when the buffer is completely) Tj
T*
(   full or upon explicit fflush\(\)/fclose\(\). Default for disk files.) Tj
T*
(   - _IOLBF \(Line Buffering\): Flushes buffer when a newline \('\\n'\) is encountered. Default for) Tj
T*
(   interactive terminals \(stdout\).) Tj
T*
(   - _IONBF \(No Buffering\): Bytes are transmitted immediately to kernel without buffering. Default) Tj
T*
(   for stderr.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The setvbuf\(\) Prototype:) Tj
T*
(   int setvbuf\(FILE *stream, char *buffer, int mode, size_t size\);) Tj
T*
(   Must be called immediately after fopen\(\) before any I/O occurs!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
