(   [Problem Statement]: Explain memory-mapped file access vs standard buffered stream I/O in large-scale bioinformatics data pipelines.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Memory-Mapped Files \(mmap\) vs Stream I/O:) Tj
T*
(   1. Standard Stream I/O \(fread/fwrite\):) Tj
T*
(   - Reads data from disk into OS Page Cache, then copies bytes into user-space buffer \(Double) Tj
T*
(   Buffering\).) Tj
T*
(   - Incurs memory copy overhead and syscall latency for multi-gigabyte genomics files.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Memory-Mapped Files \(mmap / CreateFileMapping\):) Tj
T*
(   - Maps disk file blocks directly into process virtual memory address space.) Tj
T*
(   - Access file contents via standard memory pointers \(char *ptr = mmap\(...\)\).) Tj
T*
(   - Demand Paging: Operating system pages in 4 KB blocks on-demand when memory addresses are) Tj
T*
(   dereferenced.) Tj
T*
(   - Zero-Copy: Eliminates intermediate memory copying. Ideal for random access indexing of large) Tj
T*
(   genome assemblies.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
