(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the endianness problem when transmitting binary structures across network sockets or disk files with code remedies.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Endianness Independence in Binary Serialization:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Problem:) Tj
T*
(   Little-Endian architectures \(x86/x64\) store least significant bytes at lower addresses.) Tj
T*
(   Big-Endian architectures \(SPARC, Network protocols\) store most significant bytes at lower) Tj
T*
(   addresses.) Tj
T*
(   Writing raw binary structs to disk breaks when read by systems with opposite endianness!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Serialization Remedy:) Tj
T*
(   Always serialize data in network byte order \(Big-Endian\) using standard conversion functions:) Tj
T*
(   - htons\(\) / ntohs\(\): 16-bit integers.) Tj
T*
(   - htonl\(\) / ntohl\(\): 32-bit integers.) Tj
T*
(   Or serialize byte-by-byte with explicit bitwise shifts:) Tj
T*
(   void write_uint32_be\(FILE *fp, unsigned int val\) {) Tj
T*
(   fputc\(\(val >> 24\) & 0xFF, fp\);) Tj
T*
(   fputc\(\(val >> 16\) & 0xFF, fp\);) Tj
T*
(   fputc\(\(val >> 8\) & 0xFF, fp\);) Tj
T*
(   fputc\(val & 0xFF, fp\);) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
